  "tibetan_transliteration": "\u0f66\u0fb3\u0f7c\u0f56\u0f0b\u0f51\u0f54\u0f7c\u0f53\u0f0b\u0f5e\u0f72\u0f0b\u0f56\u0f0b\u0f63\u0fb7\u0f0b\u0f53\u0f66\u0f0b\u0f56\u0f62\u0f92\u0fb1\u0f74\u0f51\u0f0b\u0f54\u0f60\u0f72\u0f0b\u0f56\u0fb1\u0f44\u0f0b\u0f46\u0f74\u0f56\u0f0b\u0f40\u0fb1\u0f72\u0f0b\u0f66\u0f7a\u0f58\u0f66\u0f0b\u0f66\u0fa6\u0fb1\u0f7c\u0f44\u0f0b\u0f56",
  "comments": "The colophon at the end of the last instruction specifies that the text was written down at the occasion of a teaching by sKyo ston (ID:4052) to Chos kyi rgyal mtshan (ID:6458), without specifying the identity of the scribe. \r\nThe post-colophon annotation identifying the scribe as Chos kyi rgyal mtshan can either refer to this process of writing \"in a master copy\" (phyag dep dngos la bris) for sKyo ston, but could also refer to a subsequent copy \"based on the master copy\" (reading: phyag dpe dngos  las bris).",
